Dataset scsi_client {
  label: 'SCSI Collections (Client-Scoped)'
  description: '''
Debt-collections demo dataset for SCSI, built on the `demo_scsi_nam` data source.

## What this dataset can answer
- Recovery performance: amount collected, liquidation rate (payments ÷ original balance), and how both vary by client, industry, collector, team and aging bucket.
- Portfolio composition: placed volume and balance by client, status and vintage.
- Collections activity: contact attempt volume, channel mix and outcomes, and how activity relates to recovery.

## Grain and joins
`public_accounts` is the central model, at one row per placed account. `public_payments` and
`public_contact_attempts` are both finer than that — an account can have many of each — so joining
either one fans out account rows. Count `public_accounts.account_id` distinctly whenever those
models are in the query.

## Date lenses
Three different dates mean three different things and must not be mixed silently:
- `accounts.placement_date` — when the account entered collections (vintage/cohort lens)
- `payments.payment_date` — when cash was received (recovery lens)
- `contact_attempts.attempt_date` — when the agency worked the account (activity lens)

Always state which lens a figure uses.

## Caveats
This is synthetic demo data. `original_balance` is fixed at placement and does not amortise, so
remaining balance is not directly available — derive it as original balance minus payments received.
'''
  data_source_name: 'demo_scsi_nam'

  models: [
    public_accounts,
    public_clients,
    public_collectors,
    public_contact_attempts,
    public_payments
  ]

  relationships: [
    relationship(public_accounts.client_id > public_clients.client_id, true),
    relationship(public_accounts.collector_id > public_collectors.collector_id, true),
    relationship(public_contact_attempts.account_id > public_accounts.account_id, true),
    relationship(public_payments.account_id > public_accounts.account_id, true)
  ]

  // Row-level scoping for the external client portal: a provider organisation sees only
  // the accounts it placed with the agency. SCSI collects medical debt, so these rows are
  // PHI and the agency is a HIPAA Business Associate of the placing provider.
  //
  // DEMO-GRADE ATTRIBUTE — MUST CHANGE BEFORE ANY REAL PHI LANDS HERE.
  // Scoped on `company_id` because that is what exists in this demo tenant today.
  //
  // `company_id` is ALREADY BOUND to Brainstorm APAC's company_access permission across
  // three datasets (brainstorm_adoption, brainstorm_cases, brainstorm_speakup) on an
  // unrelated id-space. A user provisioned for Brainstorm who reaches this dataset would
  // silently resolve to a different organisation's rows — no error, no signal. That is
  // acceptable for a controlled demo and NOT acceptable for production PHI.
  //
  // Production fix: create a dedicated `scsi_client_id` user attribute (Settings → User
  // Attributes), then change `value:` below to 'scsi_client_id'. A dedicated attribute
  // removes the cross-dataset collision and keeps "who was scoped to what, and why"
  // answerable in a BAA audit.
  //
  // Scoping on accounts (not clients) is deliberate: payments and contact_attempts inherit
  // the restriction through their joins to accounts, so no child row escapes it.
  //
  // Not enforced for Admins or Analysts with data source access, so internal staff keep
  // full agency visibility on scsi_collections_analytics.
  // NOTE: RLS-as-code must be enabled for this tenant and only takes effect after publish.
  permission client_scope {
    field: r(public_accounts.client_id)
    operator: 'matches_user_attribute'
    value: 'company_id'
  }
}
